---

## 8. Business Logic

### Menu loading

```text
Fetch products
    ↓
Sort by category/name
    ↓
Store in client state
    ↓
Render categories + products
```

### Add to cart

```text
Click Add to Cart
    ↓
Find product by ID
    ↓
Check availability
    ↓
Increment existing quantity OR add new line item
    ↓
Re-render cart
```

### Reward

```text
Cart subtotal < ₹250
    → no reward

Cart subtotal ≥ ₹250
    → reward available
    → tap reveal
    → select available reward
    → display free reward
    → include reward in WhatsApp message
```

### Checkout

```text
Cart
 ↓
Calculate subtotal
 ↓
Add ₹20 delivery
 ↓
Append free reward when applicable
 ↓
Build text message
 ↓
Open WhatsApp
```

---

## 9. Why WhatsApp Was Used Instead of Full Checkout

This was a deliberate simplification.

A full ecommerce system would introduce:

- payment integration
- order database/state management
- order confirmation
- customer identity/contact management
- order-status tracking
- business-side order dashboard

For a small cafe whose existing workflow already runs through WhatsApp, that additional infrastructure can be unnecessary early on.

The website therefore concentrates on the parts that create immediate value:

**discover → choose → calculate → send order**

---

## 10. Product Evolution Summary

The project can be understood as this progression:

```text
Static menu
   ↓
Database-backed menu
   ↓
Category navigation
   ↓
Search
   ↓
Cart
   ↓
Delivery calculation
   ↓
WhatsApp ordering
   ↓
Order-value incentive
   ↓
Customer reviews
   ↓
Product imagery
   ↓
Analytics / live deployment
```

That progression is important because it shows the project did not begin as an oversized architecture. Features were added as the business use-case became clearer.

---

## 11. Known Constraints / Honest Limitations

The site is a lightweight ordering system, not a full restaurant POS or delivery-management platform.

Current limitations include:

- WhatsApp is the final order channel.
- The browser-side cart is session-local rather than a persistent server-side cart.
- There is no observed integrated payment gateway in the current code artifacts.
- There is no observed delivery tracking system.
- The mystery reward logic is client-side and is intended as an engagement mechanic, not an anti-fraud promotion engine.
- Analytics volume can be too small to draw strong conclusions from short time windows.

These are trade-offs, not necessarily defects, for the current size of the business.

---

## 12. Analytics Notes

The live site is monitored through Vercel Analytics.

A recent snapshot showed that very small visitor counts can produce extreme percentage changes in bounce rate and traffic. For example, a 7-day period with only a handful of visitors is not a statistically meaningful basis for judging conversion quality.

For this reason, future analytics review should focus on:

- visitor count
- traffic source
- engagement
- menu interaction
- cart interaction
- WhatsApp checkout clicks
- actual cafe orders

rather than relying on bounce rate alone.

